<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · Celebration · 1847 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">O Holy Night</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="fr">Cantique de Noël (Minuit, chrétiens)</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Christmas song written in France in 1847 for a village Midnight Mass, now sung all over the world.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Placide Cappeau; English version by John Sullivan Dwight</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Adolphe Adam</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>French Catholics; later Christians of Canada, the United States and beyond</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>French, English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Roquemaure, France</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>poem written 3 December 1847; first performed at Midnight Mass 1847</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred · Celebration</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (French words by Placide Cappeau (1847), music by Adolphe Adam (1847); English words by John Sullivan Dwight (1855))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>After the stained glass of the parish church of Roquemaure in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> was restored, the local priest, Maurice Gilles, asked Placide Cappeau to write a new text for the coming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> Midnight Mass. Cappeau wrote the poem, &quot;Minuit, chrétiens&quot;, on 3 December 1847 during a stagecoach journey from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macon">Mâcon</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dijon">Dijon</a>. It draws on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nativity-of-jesus">Nativity of Jesus</a> and reflects his social and abolitionist views. Through his connection with the singer Emily Laurey he was introduced to the composer Adolphe Adam, then known for works such as Giselle, who set it to music within days. Cappeau called the finished work &quot;Cantique de Noël&quot;, and it was first performed at Midnight Mass in Roquemaure in 1847. The first form of its opening stanza cast the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> in a negative light against the Christians; it was replaced, in a change described as a &quot;happy correction&quot;.</p><p>The song spread quickly. An organ arrangement was published around 1855, and by 1864 the Catholic music journal Revue de Musique Sacrée reported it sung in churches at Midnight Mass and also in the streets, at social gatherings and in bars. After the revolution of 1848 the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alphonse-de-lamartine">Alphonse de Lamartine</a> called it &quot;a religious Marseillaise&quot;, a comparison that troubled some observers. Church leaders criticised its militant tone and its theology, objecting in particular to its picture of an angry Father, and it was excluded from Catholic hymnals. Much of the criticism was aimed at its makers: Cappeau for his politics, his reputation and his later break with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, and Adam through false rumours that he was Jewish, repeated as late as 1930 in an article by the composer Vincent d'Indy. In 1864 the same journal called it &quot;debased and degenerated&quot;. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the composer Auguste Sérieyx still condemned those who performed it, and by 1956 it had been removed from many dioceses.</p><p>Abroad it fared differently. The Canadian composer and folklorist Ernest Gagnon heard it at a Midnight Mass at the Church of Saint-Roch and introduced it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> in 1858, with a performance in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> by the eldest daughter of René-Édouard Caron; solo performances at Midnight Mass became a tradition there. On 22 September 1936 Archbishop Joseph-Guillaume-Laurent Forbes banned it from the churches of the Archdiocese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottawa">Ottawa</a>, along with other music, to promote Gregorian chant. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> the critic and minister John Sullivan Dwight, influenced by Ralph Waldo Emerson, wrote the best-known English version in 1855, changing the French text well beyond a direct translation. As an abolitionist he admired the song's emphasis on human equality; its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theme-system">themes</a> resonated during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a>, and his Dwight's Journal of Music helped make it popular in the North. Another English version, &quot;O Night Divine&quot;, was made by Hart Pease Danks in the late 19th century.</p><p>Accounts have gathered around it. The inventor Reginald Fessenden's violin performance of it in 1906 is often called the first song broadcast on radio, though this is not formally documented, and a legend holds that French troops sang it on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> Eve 1870 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a>, bringing a brief ceasefire. The carol historian William Studwell called it the most popular carol in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. In French and Canadian tradition it is sung as a solo at Midnight Mass, and listeners wait to hear whether the singer will reach its high climax. It has been recorded in many genres, and a 2010 survey by Zogby International ranked it the most popular Christmas song in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> hymn that hails the midnight of Christ's birth as the hour of humanity's redemption. Later verses turn to his teaching of love and to the hope that oppression and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> will end. The mood is solemn and rises to a triumphant close.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">French</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">French</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Minuit, chrétiens ! c’est l’heure solennelle,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Midnight, Christians! it is the solemn hour,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Où l’homme Dieu descendit jusqu’à nous</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>When the God-man came down to us</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Pour effacer la tache originelle,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To wipe away the original stain,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Et de son Père arrêter le courroux.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And to stop the wrath of his Father.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Le monde entier tressaille d’espérance,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The whole world thrills with hope,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">À cette nuit qui lui donne un Sauveur.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>On this night that gives it a Saviour.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Peuple à genoux,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>People, on your knees,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Attends ta délivrance,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Await your deliverance,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Noël, Noël,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Noël, Noël,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Voici le Rédempteur,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Here is the Redeemer,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Noël, Noël,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Noël, Noël,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Voici le Rédempteur.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Here is the Redeemer.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">De notre foi que la lumière ardente</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>May the burning light of our faith</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Nous guide tous au berceau de l’enfant,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Guide us all to the cradle of the child,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Comme autrefois une étoile brillante</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>As once a shining star</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Y conduisit les chefs de l’Orient.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Led there the chiefs of the East.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Le Roi des rois naît dans une humble crèche ;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The King of kings is born in a humble manger;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Puissants du jour, fiers de votre grandeur,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Mighty ones of the day, proud of your greatness,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">À votre orgueil c’est de là qu’un Dieu prêche :</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>It is from there that a God preaches to your pride:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Courbez, courbez vos fronts devant le Rédempteur,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Bow, bow your brows before the Redeemer,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Courbez vos fronts devant le Rédempteur.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Bow your brows before the Redeemer.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Le Rédempteur a brisé toute entrave</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The Redeemer has broken every shackle</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">La terre est libre et le ciel est ouvert.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The earth is free and heaven is open.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Il voit un frère où n’était qu’un esclave :</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>He sees a brother where there was only a slave:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">L’amour unit ceux qu’enchaînait le fer !</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Love unites those whom iron chained!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Qui lui dira notre reconnaissance ?</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Who will tell him our gratitude?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">C’est pour nous tous qu’il naît, qu’il souffre et meurt :</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>It is for us all that he is born, that he suffers and dies:</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Peuple, debout !</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>People, stand up!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Chante ta délivrance,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Sing your deliverance,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Noël, Noël,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Noël, Noël,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Chantons le Rédempteur,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let us sing the Redeemer,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Noël, Noël,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Noël, Noël,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="fr">Chantons le Rédempteur.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let us sing the Redeemer.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Placide Cappeau (1847). Text from <a href="https://fr.wikisource.org/wiki/Minuit_chr%C3%A9tiens" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (4)</summary><ul><li>line 1.3: 'la tache originelle', the original stain: original sin.</li><li>line 3.4: 'les chefs de l'Orient', the chiefs (leaders) of the East: the Magi.</li><li>line 3.6: 'Puissants du jour', the mighty of the present day.</li><li>'Noël' is kept as in the original: the Christmas cry.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
